SQL is the standard language for relational databases, though dialects differ. Data definition language (DDL) defines schema objects: CREATE makes tables, ALTER changes them and DROP removes them. Data manipulation language (DML) works on rows: SELECT reads, INSERT adds, UPDATE changes and DELETE removes.

Example: CREATE TABLE students (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, name VARCHAR(80));
Constraints such as PRIMARY KEY and FOREIGN KEY enforce rules. Check your DBMS manual for exact syntax.
